WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.710
Is Morbius a hero or a villain?

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:06.870
Is the movie a vampire horror film, or a superhero
movie with fangs?

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:09.880
And most importantly, is it any good?

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.910
These are the 6 best and 6 worst things about
"Morbius."

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:18.350
On paper, Jared Leto's Michael Morbius is
a pretty compelling character.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:22.920
Suffering from a rare and usually fatal blood
disease since birth, he becomes one of the

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:27.769
world's leading experts on artificial blood
and is even awarded a Nobel prize for his

00:00:27.769 --> 00:00:28.769
efforts.

00:00:28.769 --> 00:00:31.130
"You need a doctor?"

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:32.450
"I am a doctor."

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:37.421
Morbius eventually attempts to cure his condition
by splicing his own DNA with that of vampire

00:00:37.421 --> 00:00:43.870
bats, causing him to develop a number of supernatural
abilities and a deep craving for human blood.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:48.469
Most of the film deals with Morbius' internal
struggle as he tries to balance his new violent

00:00:48.469 --> 00:00:52.370
urges with the humanitarian ideals that he's
always tried to live by.

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:57.510
That should be the formula for an emotionally
intense and nuanced antihero story, and at

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.500
times, "Morbius" shows glimpses of just that.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.580
Michael has contributed a lot of good to the
world, and as the viewer, you truly believe

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.570
that his experiments are largely motivated
by a desire to help others.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:12.270
At the same time, Morbius can be a pretty
selfish man.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:16.610
Though his desire to help the world at large
is genuine, his number one motivation seems

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:18.550
to be his own quest for survival.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:23.700
There's a lot of rich potential in this tension
between selflessness and self-preservation,

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.790
and Morbius' vampire transformation could
have been used to explore the character's

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:28.790
inherent duality.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:35.150
Michael Morbius starts out with a lot of potential
for a nuanced, complex character arc.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.960
Unfortunately, the film wastes no time in
squandering that potential.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.690
Unnecessary time jumps and a poorly written
childhood backstory sequence establish the

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:47.210
character's tragic past in about as clinical
a way as possible.

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:52.630
As a result, the audience has no real emotional
connection to Morbius by the time he undergoes

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.220
his transformation.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:58.729
There's a face-value sympathy in a man who
has spent his whole life on the brink of death,

00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:03.040
but the script seems to expect that premise
to be enough, and very little time is spent

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.680
developing Michael as an actual character.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.560
Is he actually scared to die?

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.979
What is he living for?

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:10.720
What does he want to leave behind?

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.310
These are the kinds of questions that, if
they'd been addressed on screen, could have

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:19.069
made for a deeply textured character — perhaps
one more deserving of Jared Leto's bizarre

00:02:19.069 --> 00:02:20.470
method acting techniques.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:25.440
Instead, viewers are graced with a stoic protagonist
with excruciatingly little substance.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:30.540
One of the best things "Morbius" has going
for it is that the whole aesthetic surrounding

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:33.000
the central character looks pretty cool.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.500
He has long vampire hair.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.230
He wears a black vampire trench coat.

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:40.870
And when he fights, he flies around with a
lightning-quick fighting style that's accentuated

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.870
by some very stylish smoky swirly effects.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:49.019
Some might argue that Morbius' powers rely
too heavily on CGI, but for the most part,

00:02:49.019 --> 00:02:50.830
that CGI does its job well.

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:54.370
"Morbius" is never better than when the Living
Vampire is in action.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:58.690
The film's first major action sequence, which
sees him take down a group of mercenaries

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:03.470
on the ship where he mutates himself, is a
definite highlight, blending the from-the-shadows

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:08.060
tension of the "Batman" movies with the hard-hitting
speed of Zack Snyder's Superman.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:12.269
The film never explains whether the smoke
trails that accentuate Morbius' movements

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:17.349
are a side-effect of his transformation, a
result of his incredible speed, or what, but

00:03:17.349 --> 00:03:19.400
whatever it is, it looks pretty cool.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:24.700
The special effects culminate in a genuinely
striking scene where Morbius uses his echolocation

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:30.200
to find a single heartbeat in New York City,
then flies to it in a whirlwind of purple

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.200
and black.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.510
It's honestly shocking that Leto looks so
convincing as Morbius, considering that he's

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:37.180
nearly twice the character's age.

00:03:38.569 --> 00:03:42.659
While the stylish aesthetic created around
the Morbius character is quite effective,

00:03:42.659 --> 00:03:46.769
the rest of the movie suffers from a myriad
of visual problems.

00:03:46.769 --> 00:03:51.220
Cinematographer Oliver Wood is a seasoned
veteran of his craft, and it would be irresponsible

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:55.860
to hold him solely responsible for the camera
work in "Morbius," but a certain amount of

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.689
blame does inevitably fall at his feet.

00:03:58.689 --> 00:04:02.860
Wood is perhaps most famous for his work on
contemporary action movies like "Die Hard

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:05.709
2," "Face/Off," and the original "Bourne"
trilogy.

00:04:05.709 --> 00:04:10.189
Though the "Bourne" films received critical
acclaim, they've also become notorious in

00:04:10.189 --> 00:04:13.580
the years since for popularizing the use of
shaky cam.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:18.250
Not every issue with the cinematography in
"Morbius" is due to shaky cam, but the technique

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:22.500
is indicative of the larger problems plaguing
the film's cinematic style.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.259
Nearly every scene is shot with the same level
of intensity.

00:04:25.259 --> 00:04:27.440
At best, it's mildly nauseating.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.830
At worst, it's practically unwatchable.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:35.590
Modern superhero movies seem obsessed with
competing for the longest runtime.

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:39.780
Films like "Avengers: Endgame," "Spider-Man:
No Way Home," and "The Batman" all clock in

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:44.870
around the two-and-a-half to three hour range,
which can be a big ask for moviegoers.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:49.060
Of course, all those films were also very
well received upon release, so it could be

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:52.470
argued that they needed their extra length
to pull everything together.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:57.050
Still, it's refreshing to see a superhero
movie that keeps things short and sweet.

00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:01.970
"Morbius" is listed at an hour and 44 minutes,
and it definitely never dwells too long on

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:03.030
any one scene.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:07.360
It's a fast and furious movie, and not just
because of the inclusion of Tyrese Gibson.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.050
"Mmm hmm, this is crazy man."

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:11.070
"What're you talking about now?"

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:13.389
"I'm sitting there looking at these images."

00:05:13.389 --> 00:05:18.120
In fact, if you compare the final film to
the trailers, you'll see that lots of scenes

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.430
and lines were removed for the final cut.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:24.050
If sitting through the three hours that a
lot of other movies demand these days is a

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:28.240
dealbreaker for you, then you may be glad
to see how brief "Morbius" truly is.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:33.770
Despite its relatively short runtime, "Morbius"
still has major pacing problems.

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:37.970
It somehow manages to feel both sluggish and
rushed at the same time.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:42.860
The film opens with a beautiful vista shot
of Costa Rica, where Michael Morbius has traveled

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:44.729
to capture vampire bats.

00:05:44.729 --> 00:05:49.900
After a brief shot of him using his own blood
as bait, the film jumps back in time 25 years

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:54.150
for a stilted backstory sequence explaining
his difficult childhood.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:58.960
After that, it's another abrupt cut to present-day
New York, where Morbius is revealed to have

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.250
successfully captured a whole swarm of bats
off screen.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:06.910
This sort of rapid-fire pacing continues throughout
the film, but it gets even more distracting

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:09.639
once the vampire transformation takes place.

00:06:09.639 --> 00:06:14.430
Morbius shows very little emotion in response
to his monstrous state, with only a handful

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:17.850
of loud outbursts to augment his otherwise
stoic demeanor.

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:22.850
If the movie ever slowed down for a second,
it might be able to humanize Morbius and his

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:27.100
struggle a bit more, but instead, the script
keeps jumping between underdeveloped side

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:30.139
characters and overwritten exposition.

00:06:30.139 --> 00:06:34.720
There's no time for viewers to catch their
breath, much less develop any tangible emotional

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:36.100
attachments.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:39.910
Perhaps worst of all, this breakneck pace
makes every section of the movie feel the

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:40.910
same.

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:46.170
The frantic camera work and abrupt scene transitions
make it impossible to determine which moments

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:52.610
are pivotal and which are supplemental, turning
the entirety of "Morbius" into one long, repetitive act.

00:06:53.617 --> 00:06:58.200
Like the vampire for which it's named, "Morbius"
is not entirely devoid of life.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:02.440
Most of the humor and humanity that can be
found in the film comes from Tyrese Gibson

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:08.080
and Al Madrigal, who play a pair of FBI agents
investigating Morbius' activities.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:13.440
The interplay between Gibson's stone-cold
persona and Madrigal's deadpan comedy is genuinely

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.930
funny, with great moments like the latter
bringing a "triple-blessed" vial of holy water

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:20.340
to their interview with the vampire.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:23.960
Madrigal and Gibson are so entertaining, in
fact, that it's easy to envision a better

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:26.860
version of "Morbius" where they play a larger
role.

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:31.870
Instead, the feds get relegated to a C-tier
supporting cast position, and their occasional

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:35.270
jokes are ultimately drowned out by the film's
cacophonous self-seriousness.

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:41.500
For as good as the FBI agents are in "Morbius,"
the other supporting characters are deeply

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:42.500
lacking.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:47.180
First and foremost, Matt Smith's Milo, based
loosely on the character Hunger from the Marvel

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:49.050
comics, is a terrible villain.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:53.430
From the moment he's introduced, it's painfully
obvious that he'll eventually turn evil.

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:57.849
When Michael tells his friend "this isn't
you," it's almost laughable; this is clearly

00:07:57.849 --> 00:08:00.400
the only thing Milo was ever written to be.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:05.410
Equally disappointing is Michael's romantic
subplot with Doctor Martine Bancroft, played

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:06.970
by Adria Arjona.

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:11.370
The actress does her best with a script that
is constantly working against her, but her

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:12.569
efforts just aren't enough.

00:08:13.569 --> 00:08:17.590
The greatest trick that "Morbius" pulls is
showing you glimpses of a better movie that

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:18.590
could have been. 

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:23.430
The opening title card fades in over a cool
synthwave graphic of hard lines and bright

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:27.289
pinks, teasing a retro aesthetic that never
comes to fruition.

00:08:27.289 --> 00:08:32.339
The hard-boiled humor of the FBI agents hints
at pulpy noir, but that tone never gets enough

00:08:32.339 --> 00:08:34.919
of a foothold to influence the rest of the
film.

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:39.650
And of course, there are plenty of dark corridors
to evoke the horror genre, which "Morbius"

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:41.050
pulls from heavily.

00:08:41.050 --> 00:08:46.252
In the moments where these stylistic touches
are strongest, "Morbius" can be a pretty fun movie.

00:08:46.252 --> 00:08:51.240
Unfortunately, the movie refuses to fully
commit to any of its more interesting aesthetics,

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:56.080
languishing mostly in dreary exposition and
ripping off scenes from better movies.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:00.490
The shot of Michael being swarmed by vampire
bats in his lab is a particularly egregious

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:05.040
instance of this, with a backing score that
elicits memories of Hans Zimmer's "Batman

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:06.269
Begins" score.

00:09:06.269 --> 00:09:10.380
Unfortunately, that's a comparison "Morbius"
would probably be better off not even attempting

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:11.380
to make.

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:16.870
"It's not who i am underneath, but what I
do that defines me."

00:09:19.029 --> 00:09:24.311
Though it occasionally evokes other genres,
"Morbius" is a superhero movie through and through.

00:09:24.311 --> 00:09:28.350
But even in that space, the film feels about
two decades too late.

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:33.880
Rather than those more recent entries in the
genre, "Morbius" is easier to compare to early-2000s

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:36.279
movies like Ben Affleck's "Daredevil."

00:09:36.279 --> 00:09:41.080
From the clunky dialogue and overall angsty
tone to the flat side characters, "Morbius"

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:46.110
is a movie that feels stuck in the past — swooping
in from an era when superhero films were bad

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:50.620
more often than they were good, and before
stars like Robert Downey Jr. proved that comic

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:54.130
book exposition didn't have to sound dorky
on camera.

00:09:54.130 --> 00:09:58.410
If "Morbius" leaned more into its retro vibe,
if it allowed more of "The Crow" in and less

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:01.430
of "Ghost Rider," perhaps Sony would have
had something.

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:06.140
Instead, we're left with a series of questions
about how a movie like "Morbius" got made

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:07.140
in 2022.

00:10:08.559 --> 00:10:12.990
Like most superhero movies of the modern era,
"Morbius" peppers some teases for the future

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:14.310
with its end credits.

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:18.630
There are two post-credits scenes in the film,
both of which focus on the return of Michael

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:22.060
Keaton's Adrian Toomes character from "Spider-Man:
Homecoming."

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:27.230
Because Keaton is a fantastic actor, and because
"Homecoming" is so beloved, it's undeniably

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:29.940
fun to see Toomes brought back into the fold.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:31.970
"At what cost?"

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:32.970
Great question!

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:36.600
While the cameo is fun at first, it also comes
with its share of problems.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:41.890
For starters, seeing him at the end of "Morbius"
mostly reminds viewers of a much better movie.

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:45.670
Then there's the matter of how he actually
appears in the "Morbius" universe, which is

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:47.600
confusing to say the least.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:52.060
The first credits scene shows the sky over
New York being fractured, clearly referencing

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:54.690
the multiverse spell from "Spider-Man: No
Way Home."

00:10:54.690 --> 00:11:00.320
However, that spell previously only brought
characters from other realities into the MCU,

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:01.779
not the other way around.

00:11:01.779 --> 00:11:04.640
So why does Toomes teleport to Michael Morbius'
world?

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:08.490
How does he get a new Vulture suit without
Chitauri technology?

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:11.769
Is there even a Spider-Man in Sony's Spider-Man
Universe?

00:11:11.769 --> 00:11:16.221
These are confusing questions to leave audiences
with at the end of a movie, but given that

00:11:16.221 --> 00:11:18.880
the movie is "Morbius," they're a welcome
distraction.

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:22.830
From top to bottom, "Morbius" is a failure.

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:27.230
It ruins all efforts at horror and tension
by rushing through every scene.

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:31.779
It kneecaps a potentially interesting protagonist
by leaving him cold and underdeveloped.

00:11:31.779 --> 00:11:38.120
It is an hour and 44 minutes of messy plotting,
clumsy dialogue, and nauseating cinematography,

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:43.713
and it ends with one of the most egregious
CGI slugfests in the history of superhero cinema.

00:11:43.713 --> 00:11:47.670
The "Venom" movies might not be masterpieces,
but they embrace their campy tone in a way

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:50.130
that at least makes them entertaining to watch.

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:54.459
"Morbius," on the other hand, is the worst
kind of bad movie — the kind that's so messy

00:11:54.459 --> 00:11:58.700
and poorly constructed that it feels like
a chore just to keep watching it.

00:11:58.700 --> 00:12:03.430
Sony clearly has high hopes for their interconnected
"Spider-Man" universe, but as the first proper

00:12:03.430 --> 00:12:08.160
expansion into that shared continuity, "Morbius"
is a colossal disappointment.

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:10.420
"This movie was a total disappointment.

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:11.490
No thought went into it.

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:13.120
No effort went into it."

